! EX: ADDRESS MONITORING OF THE SUBJECT INSTRUCTION'S LOCATION, and
! the BELOW-32 carve-out that switches it off.
!
! THE TWO RULES, Reference Manual 22-6530-2, read first-hand at 300 dpi
! (PDF page = printed page + 3):
!
!   printed p.32, address monitoring class 1: "The location of each
!   instruction to be executed with the exception of the single
!   instructions at the effective interruption address which are
!   executed when an interruption occurs."  A subject instruction IS
!   executed, so its location is monitored -- and the indicator for an
!   instruction location is IF, indicator 21.
!
!   printed p.50, EXECUTE: "An effective address of EXECUTE that is
!   below 32.00 (including addresses between 1.00 and 3.32) is not
!   monitored by the address monitoring mechanism; that is, indicator
!   IF cannot be actuated by these addresses."
!
!   printed p.50 again, and this is why the first rule holds even for a
!   machine that has been disabled: "In general, when the interruption
!   system is disabled, interruption cannot occur and address
!   monitoring is suspended.  In order that control may remain entirely
!   with the main program during an execution operation, two special
!   exceptions are made.  First, address monitoring is always in force
!   during (1) EXECUTE and (2) EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT, except for
!   the address of the location of the first level pseudo-instruction
!   counter.  Second, at the completion, termination, or suppression of
!   an execution operation, interruption is always permitted."
!
!   printed p.51 corroborates that the SUBJECT LOCATION is an IF
!   address and not an AD one, in the one place the manual compares
!   them: "c. An effective branch address that is both invalid and
!   protected will cause only the actuation of indicator AD.  Indicator
!   IF will remain unchanged because an instruction has not been
!   fetched.  d. If the address of the location of the subject
!   instruction of EXECUTE or EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT is both
!   protected and invalid, indicator IF will be actuated.  Indicator AD
!   will not be actuated as a result of this invalid address unless the
!   IF mask bit is zero."  A subject instruction IS fetched, which is
!   what flips the precedence.  The AD half of that pair is not
!   implemented -- this model raises AD for neither -- and docs/NEXT.md
!   section 5a already records it as an open rule.
!
! NEITHER OF THOSE TWO "SPECIAL EXCEPTIONS" IS IMPLEMENTED, and this
! file does not pretend otherwise: it runs ENABLED throughout, so
! p.32's ordinary enable gate is satisfied and the question of what a
! DISABLED execution operation monitors never arises.  The gap is
! recorded in StretchExecute.bsv and in docs/NEXT.md; a program for it
! would need the model to carry an "execution operation in progress"
! state through protViolates and through the interruption test, which
! is its own piece of work.
!
! THE BLOCKS:
!
!   A  POSITIVE CONTROL.  A subject instruction whose LOCATION is
!      inside the monitored area, at a word above 32.  IF must come on.
!      Without this block, block B passes for the trivial reason that
!      nothing signals IF for a subject location at all.
!   B  THE CARVE-OUT.  The same shape with the subject instruction
!      planted in WORD 1 -- p.31's permanently protected area, "1.00 and
!      3.32" in p.50's own words.  IF must stay OFF, and the subject
!      must still execute: p.50 says "subject instructions of EXECUTE
!      may occupy any addressable location", which is exactly what
!      separates EX from EXIC ("addresses 32.00 and greater").
!   C  THE PROGRAMMING NOTE, printed p.50: "The instruction EXECUTE
!      also executes instructions conveniently from the registers.
!      This facilitates table look-up, diagnostic, and other
!      routines."  The subject here is held in index register 10, word
!      26 -- below 32, and reached through the ordinary special-storage
!      routing rather than core.
!   D  TWO LEVELS, with only the FIRST subject location monitored.  The
!      alarm from an intermediate level has to be carried forward, and
!      this is the only block that can tell carrying from overwriting.
!
! WHY WORD 1 AND NOT WORD 2 OR 3.  All three are p.31's permanently
! protected words and all three make protViolates signal, so any of
! them would drive block B.  Word 3 holds the boundary registers this
! file needs; word 2 is the interruption address, and clobbering it
! would make any interruption that did fire land somewhere arbitrary.
! Word 1 is the interval timer and clock, which this model never steps
! (StretchStorage.bsv: a plain register, written only by writeWord), so
! it is a free 64 bits that is nevertheless permanently protected.
!
! THE PLANTING IS DONE WHILE DISABLED, for the reason
! programs/df_bb_readonly.strap gives: p.32, "when the interruption
! system is disabled, address monitoring is ineffective", which is what
! lets a program store into a word it could not store into afterwards.
!
! IF'S MASK BIT IS ZERO (the reset value) throughout, so IF sets its
! indicator and interrupts nothing and suppresses nothing -- printed
! p.45 item 21: "If the mask bit was zero, the instruction was executed
! normally."  That is what makes IF readable with a plain BRANCH ON BIT
! here instead of an interrupt handler.  Of the three address alarms,
! DS is the one whose mask bit cannot be zero -- it is indicator 19 and
! p.48 makes mask bits 0-19 permanently one -- so an actuated DS always
! interrupts.  That is why no block below stores into the monitored
! area while enabled: every subject here writes to a marker word
! OUTSIDE it, and only the subject's own LOCATION is inside.
!
! THREE INJECTIONS, EACH OBSERVED (not predicted).  Each was applied to
! a pristine snapshot of bsv/StretchExecute.bsv by an exact-substring
! substitution that aborts unless the anchor occurs exactly once, and
! the resulting unified diff was printed before the run -- because a
! `sed` that silently fails to match prints ALL TESTS PASSED, which
! reads exactly like an honest "this does not discriminate".  Reproduce
! any row with `python3 line_addresses.py programs/ex_monitor.strap`.
!
!   the ifViolates term dropped from the   -> 6677  006412.40
!     carried alarm, so no subject               BZB, $IF, $   (A)
!     location signals class 1 at all            -- IF never came on
!   ifViolates -> protViolates, dropping   -> 6684  006416.00
!     p.50's below-32 carve-out                  BB, $IF, $    (B)
!                                                -- IF came on for a
!                                                   subject at word 1
!   the carried alarm OVERWRITTEN rather   -> 6698  006425.00
!     than OR-ed at each level                   BZB, $IF, $   (D)
!                                                -- the first level's
!                                                   alarm was forgotten
!
! Every one halted, none survived, and the clean run's PASS is at
! half-word 6705 (006430.40).
!
! WHICH CHECKS NO ROW REACHES, said rather than left to be found.  The
! second injection halts in block B, so block C's own "BB,$IF,$" at
! 6691 is never the halt: the register case is covered for EXECUTION
! (MARKC) but its IF check is belt-and-braces behind B's.  And no
! injection here drives the MARKP/MARKB/MARKC/MARKD memory checks on
! their own -- they fail as a consequence of halting early, which is
! why the PC is the row that names the finding.
!
! Instructions used: BD, BE, LVI, LX, SX, SV, BB, BBZ, BZB, Z, EX, B,
! BEW -- all implemented.

IT      SYN, 1.0                   ! word 1: interval timer / clock
!
        SLC, (8)6400.0
START   BD,  $+.32                 ! disabled: the set-up plants a word
                                   !   into the permanently protected area
        LX,  $X9,  SUBW            ! the half-word "Z,MARKB" in a word
        SX,  $X9,  IT              ! ... planted in word 1
        LX,  $X10, SUBW2           ! the half-word "Z,MARKC" in a register
        LVI, $X13, PROT
        SV,  $X13, $LB             ! lower boundary := PROT
        LVI, $X13, ABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB             ! upper boundary := ABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0           ! boundary control := 0, signal INSIDE
        BB1, MARKP.0, $+1.0        ! plant a one in each marker, so that
        BB1, MARKB.0, $+1.0        !   "it went to zero" means the subject
        BB1, MARKC.0, $+1.0        !   really stored
        BB1, MARKD.0, $+1.0
        BE,  $+.32                 ! ENABLE: monitoring is effective now
!
! ---- A. subject inside the monitored area, above 32: IF ----------
        Z,   $IND                  ! start from IF off
        EX,  SUBJP                 ! SUBJP lives inside [PROT, ABOVE)
        BZB, $IF,  $               ! hang if IF did not come on
        BZB, MARKP.0, A1           ! ... and the subject still executed:
        B,   $                     !     p.45 item 21, mask zero, "the
                                   !     instruction was executed normally"
!
! ---- B. subject at word 1: the carve-out, IF must stay off -------
A1      Z,   $IND                  ! clear IF (indicator 21 is settable)
        EX,  IT                    ! THE TEST: a subject below 32.00
        BB,  $IF,  $               ! hang if IF came on
        BZB, MARKB.0, B1           ! ... and it still executed: "subject
        B,   $                     !     instructions of EXECUTE may occupy
                                   !     any addressable location"
!
! ---- C. subject held in an index register (p.50's programming note)
B1      Z,   $IND
        EX,  $X10                  ! word 26: below 32, and not core at all
        BB,  $IF,  $               ! hang if IF came on
        BZB, MARKC.0, C1
        B,   $                     ! hang: the register subject did not run
!
! ---- D. two levels, and only the FIRST one is monitored ----------
!   "EX,SUBJ2A" where SUBJ2A is itself "EX,SUBJ2B".  SUBJ2A lives
!   INSIDE the monitored area; SUBJ2B lives outside it.  Printed p.50
!   says monitoring is in force "during EXECUTE" without qualifying
!   which level, and p.32 class 1 covers the location of every
!   instruction to be executed, so the first level's alarm has to
!   survive to the end of the chain.  It cannot survive by itself: an
!   EXECUTE that bails for its subject word has its indicator word
!   thrown away (StretchExecute.bsv's execute() tail), so the E-box
!   carries the alarm forward from level to level instead.  This block
!   is what catches a model that OVERWRITES that carried flag at each
!   level rather than accumulating it -- a mistake no single-level
!   block can see.
C1      Z,   $IND
        EX,  SUBJ2A
        BZB, $IF,  $               ! hang if the FIRST level's location
                                   !   was forgotten
        BZB, MARKD.0, D1
        B,   $                     ! hang: the chain did not finish
D1      BD,  $+.32                 ! disable before halting
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the subject held in core, above 32 and inside the area ------
        SLC, (8)6701.0
SUBJP   Z,   MARKP                 ! its LOCATION is monitored; MARKP, the
                                   !   word it stores into, is not -- a
                                   !   store into the area would actuate
                                   !   DS, whose mask bit is always one
!
!   Block D's first level, also inside the area.  Its own subject is
!   SUBJ2B, which is outside it.
        SLC, (8)6703.0
SUBJ2A  EX,  SUBJ2B
!
! ---- the subject words that get planted below 32 -----------------
        SLC, (8)6600.0
SUBW    Z,   MARKB                 ! copied into word 1 by the set-up
        SLC, (8)6601.0
SUBW2   Z,   MARKC                 ! loaded into index register 10
        SLC, (8)6602.0
SUBJ2B  Z,   MARKD                 ! block D's second level, OUTSIDE the
                                   !   monitored area
!
! ---- data, all of it OUTSIDE the monitored area ------------------
        SLC, (8)6500.0
MARKP   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6501.0
MARKB   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6502.0
MARKC   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6503.0
MARKD   DD(BU,64,8), 0
!
! ---- the monitored area, [PROT, ABOVE) ---------------------------
        SLC, (8)6700.0
PROT    DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! == the lower boundary
        SLC, (8)6704.0
ABOVE   DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! == the upper boundary, first word out
        END, START
